  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Friday...\n\nSatellite imagery shows a weak shortwave pushing west-to-east thru\ncentral MT, and in fact post-frontal showers have somewhat\noverachieved in NW portions of the forecast area this morning.\nRainfall amounts of 0.02-0.08\" have been measured in northern\nSweet Grass, Wheatland and Golden Valley Counties as of 1630z.\nIsolated to scattered mainly light showers will continue to spread\neastward this afternoon and evening. Tonight into early Saturday,\nthis will evolve into some showers along a mid level warm front\nover southeast MT, as we start to build heights behind this \ninitial wave. The precipitation will be welcomed but not \nsignificant.\n\nThe other item to note on satellite imagery is upslope low clouds\nforming along N-NE aspects of the Beartooth and Pryor Mountains,\nwith ceilings of 3000-4000 ft agl. As upslope flow deepens this\nstratus should continue to expand, and will be a modest issue with\nregard to aviation in the short term period. The persistent \neasterly winds should keep some component of low stratus into\nSunday morning, with perhaps fog along the immediate eastern\nslopes tonight and again Saturday night. Will be something to\nmonitor. Also, as a result of the upslope winds, cloud cover and\npoor mixing, have confidently dropped forecast high temps a few \ndegrees below the typical guidance.\n\nAs has been the trend the past few days, the developing low over\nthe PacNW is expected to track well to our W/NW this weekend, just\nclipping far northwest MT before entering southern AB late Sunday.\nThis is a dry track for our forecast area and pop/QPF forecasts\nreflect a continued downward trend. Temps will be cold enough for\nhigh elevation snow showers over the western mountains Sunday \nnight into Monday, but the air mass is looking dry during this \ntime period so snow is looking like a non-issue.\n\nAll of that said, there may be a risk of strong t-storms over\nsoutheast MT Sunday night we see theta-e return in a pre-frontal \nenvironment. There remains uncertainty with regard to where a\nsurface low develops (consensus is for north central WY) and how\nmuch surface heating we see on Sunday, but the overall convective\nsignals suggest this is something to watch. With or without\nt-storms, scattered pops should suffice in the east Sunday night\nwith pwats rising to 1+\" giving support for localized moderate to\nheavy rainfall, albeit briefly. As a side note, 700mb plume to\n+12C would support surface temps into the 80s in our south if SW\nwinds are mixed into (do not think this will happen). Regarding \nfire wx, the increasing pre-frontal mid level SW winds will likely\nresult in elevated fire concerns over the high elevations of the \nGallatin NF.\n\nMonday will be dry, cooler & breezy behind a Pacific cold frontal\npassage Sunday night. Given the track of the closed low to our \nnorth the wind signal for Monday has strengthened, with ensembles \nshowing gusts ~50 mph along the western foothills, and 30-40 mph \nfurther east. Do not see enough mid level flow for wind highlights\nat this time, but gusty crosswinds may impact travel along I-90 \nand US-191. Look for temps in the 60s to lower 70s Monday. Lighter\nwinds and clear skies may yield frost in notoriously colder spots\nMonday night. Probabilities for a low temp of 36F or colder is \n20-40% at these locations.\n\nNW flow behind the departing low will keep temps a little cooler\nthan normal through midweek. Another shortwave dropping out of\nwestern Canada brings a chance (15-25%) of light precip Tuesday\nnight and Wednesday, but models are in strong disagreement in\ntiming and strength of a midweek shortwave. Higher heights should\npush temps back to the 70s or near 80F late week, but another weak\ntrof from the west may bring more shower potential by Friday.\n\nJKL",
